A||||10||NASAReeves||EGR Tube Removal||||13||
Z||000000||NASAReeves||05-11-2001||10:38 PM||||Hello, Everyone.  I'm a newby to the forum.  Everyone here seems really seasoned, and I'm hoping someone can help me.  <P>We have: '88 GT, V-6.  We also have: the classic broken EGR Tube, pushing the idle to 2000rpm+.  I ordered the new one (along with Rodney's steel vacuum tubes), and am ready to install it all...but...<P>How the heck to I get the old tube off of the plenum/manifold?  You guys might laugh, but I'm too new at this to attempt removing the plenum.  I'd have to have AAA tow it to Ed Parks for sure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  And to be honest with you, I can't figure out how to pull the ignition so that it's out of the way for my wrench.  I think I read somewhere that it was a 10mm, but I'm not even sure of that (can't get it in there).  Anybody's advice would be greatly appreciated.<P>Thank you in advance!<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||13
Z||000001||voyagerspe||05-12-2001||01:56 AM||||ok <BR>this is how you do it<P>1. go buy a chiltons or haynes manual for your car(or get both)<BR>2. read it cover to cover<BR>3. get a 12 point 10 mm socket and a 10 mm wrench and a torx bit for the throttle body.<BR>4. take off the manifold(dont be scared)<BR>5. replace the vacuumelines 1 at a time<BR>6. replace the egr pipe<BR>7. install manifold<P>Its that easy, just take your time.<BR>some other hints <P>remove the deck lid over the engine (get a friend to help)<BR>label things with tape and/or lay things out on a bench next to the car. <P>good luck<BR> <P><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000002||TOM||05-12-2001||02:14 PM||||And heres what I say. DONT remove the decklid. People always want to remove the deck lid. Even for simple things like changing the spark plugs. AND DONT remove the plenum. I have changed numerous egr tubes and vacuum lines on many fieros and I dont remove either of these. Dont make jobs longer or more complicated than they need to be. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Stan86SE||05-12-2001||03:43 PM||||Scott,<P>I agree with voyagerspe.  I was just like you...I thought maybe this was a job that I didn't want to try to handle.  But, if you're at 'all' mechanically inclined, you'll find that it's basically a piece of cake.<P>Like voyagerspe said, get a Haynes or Chilton manual, follow the instructions on removing the plenum, replace one vacuum line at a time, replace the EGR tube, reinstall the plenum and you're home free.  Another good tip is to take a piece of cardboard, and punch holes in it corresponding to the pattern of the bolts that you remove.  As you remove each bolt, put it in the corresponding hole in the cardboard...that way you'll automatically know where each bolt goes when you reassemble everything.<P>With just a little patience, you'll be surprised at just how simple it is to do what you want done.<P>Good luck and welcome to the forum!<P>- Stan <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Stan86SE||05-12-2001||03:49 PM||||Scott,<P>P.S.  I also agree with TOM, in that I personally see no reason to have to remove the decklid.  'Course, I have an '86 SE, if that makes any difference?<P>- Stan<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||TOM||05-12-2001||06:29 PM||||I need to ask WHY remove the plenum and go through the work of cleaning two gasket surfaces and torquing the manifold if you dont need to. Im not trying to be a hole I just dont understand.||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||05-12-2001||10:25 PM||||I found it very easy to remove the EGR tube after removing the distributor.  Just mark the base carefully before removing it.  Then just remove the two bolts on the EGR tube. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||voyagerspe||05-12-2001||11:51 PM||||I dont know how you could replace all the vacumme lines without removing the top manifold.  Ive personnal installed the rodney 's steel vac lines and there is NO WAY that you could install them properly if you did not remove the plenum.  The egr tube can be replace without removing the plenum but not the vac lines.  Also removing the decklid is not nessisary but it takes 2 minutes and it just makes the job easier.<P>ok that my 2 cents<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||TOM||05-13-2001||03:18 PM||||Ok I guess if your installing steel lines then you would need to. But being an aricraft mechanic we do alot of things in very little space.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spektyr||05-13-2001||03:57 PM||||The first time changing my plugs I yanked the decklid off.  Was a lot easier to do my WWF plenum head-lock maneuver that way.<P>Of course I probably won't need to do that next time since I won't have the plugs in so long that they rust.<P>And I'll remember to blow the crud out of there BEFORE I start...<P>Removing the decklid is an easy 1-man job, could probably be replaced with only one guy, but having a helper makes it a cinch.<P>If I have the time, and I'm new to the car, I'll take all sorts of things apart.  It helps me get familiar with the car.<P>"Oh, so THAT'S where that vacuum line comes from..."  etc, etc||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||05-13-2001||09:27 PM||||I just replaced my EGR tube without removing the plenum or the distributor. If you remove the cap and rotor from the distributor, you'll have plenty of room. Also unplug the connectors from the TPS and the IAC (both on the throttle body), and swing the cable out of the way. The plenum end of the EGR tube is held on by two 10 mm bolts. The EGR end of the tube uses 10 mm nuts. Use liquid wrench on the nuts on the EGR end. It will make them come out easier. On both of the tubes I have replaced, one of the studs backed out of the EGR, instead of the nut coming loose. Not a big deal.<BR>Of course this isn't going to be a lot of help with the replacement vacuum lines. You MAY have to remove the plenum for that.<BR>Most cars have a schematic diagram of all the vacuum plumbing, on a sticker on the underside of the decklid. Have fun. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
